WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
Moscow has stepped up a pre-winter campaign&nbsp;
to strike civilian infrastructure in Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:10.020
using missiles and drones as NATO members&nbsp;
promise to send Kyiv more air defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.520
systems. My guest this week on Conflict Zone&nbsp;
from the Berlin Foreign Policy Forum is NATO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:20.280
Secretary General Jens Stoltenberg. How far&nbsp;
can NATO members increased support for Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:26.100
go to help Kyiv repel Putin’s invasion?
“The systems NATO allies and partners have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:31.500
delivered have already made a huge difference.&nbsp;
This is one of the reasons why Russia has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.480
not been able to gain air superiority,&nbsp;
control over the airspace over Ukraine.”
 &nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.420
As Putin issues veiled nuclear threats, how&nbsp;
strong is NATO‘s deterrence today? And how well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.280
is it positioned to confront security threats&nbsp;
of the future, including those posed by China?

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.320
Secretary General Stoltenberg,&nbsp;
welcome to Conflict Zone.&nbsp;

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.500
Thank you so much for having me, Sarah.
In the past week, Ukrainian cities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.100
far from the front lines, including&nbsp;
the capital Kyiv, have been waking up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:08.220
to deadly Russian airstrikes, which appear to&nbsp;
be targeting infrastructure in civilian areas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.180
What do you think Putin aims&nbsp;
to achieve with these strikes?&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:22.380
I think the aim is to reduce the determination&nbsp;
of the Ukrainian people to stand up against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:28.920
brutal Russian invasion of their country. But&nbsp;
I think he will fail, as he has failed in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:34.560
the other attempts in trying to take control&nbsp;
over Ukraine since the invasion in February.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:41.400
And this demonstrates the brutality of the&nbsp;
war. It demonstrates how we see civilian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.660
casualties and now also critical&nbsp;
civilian infrastructure is attacked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:52.260
And it just also highlights the importance&nbsp;
of NATO and NATO allies and partners that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:56.400
we continue to provide support to Ukraine.
And to that end, last week, NATO members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.760
committed to send more air defense systems and&nbsp;
other aid to Ukraine. Do you think the new systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.740
will be enough to stop the onslaught of Russian&nbsp;
attacks with long-range weapons, including drones?&nbsp;

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:16.260
I think that the systems we are now providing&nbsp;
are making a huge difference. They have already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:24.720
made a difference in shooting down a lot&nbsp;
of the Russian missiles, drones. But also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:31.260
we will make even more difference in the&nbsp;
future because allies are now stepping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:36.900
up. We have Germany, who has just delivered a&nbsp;
new, very advanced, modern air defense systems.&nbsp;

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.120
Right.
And during the NATO defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:45.900
ministerial last week, we had several allies — the&nbsp;
United States and also others — announcing more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:52.020
systems, but also more ammunition and supplies to&nbsp;
existing systems. And this is making a difference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:57.180
The Ukrainians are able to shoot down&nbsp;
many of the incoming missiles and drones.&nbsp;

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.380
You say that they'll make a difference in&nbsp;
the future. What does the future mean to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:05.700
you? Because President Zelenskyy says 30% of&nbsp;
Ukraine's power stations have been destroyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:11.280
in these attacks. The concern is winter is&nbsp;
quickly approaching. People could struggle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.140
to heat their homes. How quickly can the systems&nbsp;
get there, especially given — and you've admitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:23.400
this — there are production shortages in the West.
Well, the systems NATO allies and partners have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:29.040
delivered have already made a huge difference.&nbsp;
This is one of the reasons why Russia has not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:35.280
been able to gain air superiority, control over&nbsp;
the airspace over Ukraine. And that has been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:41.340
big disadvantage for the Russian troops throughout&nbsp;
the operation. They expected that they were going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.320
to take control of the airspace. They have not&nbsp;
been able to take control of the airspace over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:51.600
Ukraine. This is, of course, first and foremost&nbsp;
because of the skills, the determination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:58.200
the Ukrainian armed forces using the air defense&nbsp;
systems they already had, old the Soviet types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:05.400
air defense. What NATO allies have done is that we&nbsp;
have provided ammunition to those old systems, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:12.480
then we are also bringing in new systems. And the&nbsp;
latest example is the German system, demonstrating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:19.500
that Germany is now actually delivering important&nbsp;
capabilities to Ukraine. And they will make then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:25.680
even more difference in the in the future. Winter&nbsp;
is coming. And that was also the main message from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.440
the defense ministerial. So we need to ramp up&nbsp;
support also everything from winter clothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:36.060
generators, tents, so we can help and ensure that&nbsp;
the Ukrainians can operate throughout the winter.&nbsp;

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.320
Let's talk a little bit more about the urgency for&nbsp;
action. And I'd like to turn to the battlefield&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:45.900
now. Ukraine, as we all know, has been recapturing&nbsp;
territory on the front lines in the east and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:50.700
the south. But there are some reports, including&nbsp;
today, that troops in the Donbas region have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:55.560
slowed almost to a halt. Russian reinforcements,&nbsp;
in the meantime, have rushed to the front line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:01.020
attempting to break Ukraine's momentum.&nbsp;
How critical do you see this moment right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:07.680
now? Not in the future. Right now for Ukraine's&nbsp;
counter-offensive as the colder months settle in.&nbsp;

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:18.360
So, wars are, by nature, unpredictable. And&nbsp;
of course, there are still a lot of Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:26.340
forces' capabilities in Ukraine. And they have&nbsp;
what we have seen over the last weeks and days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:32.280
is actually the most serious escalation of&nbsp;
the conflict since the invasion in February.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:36.960
Because if you put together their mobilization&nbsp;
and the forces they are now able to send to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:44.100
front with their partial — now, with the&nbsp;
illegal annexation of Ukrainian territory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:49.740
and the dangerous nuclear rhetoric — all of that&nbsp;
represents the biggest escalation since the war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:58.980
So, again, we need to just step up our support.&nbsp;
And of course, this support has proven extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:05.820
important because this has enabled the Ukrainians&nbsp;
to first push the Russians out of the area around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:12.180
Kyiv and then halt the Russian offensive in&nbsp;
Donbas and then launch a counteroffensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:19.080
where they have been able to regain, retake a&nbsp;
lot of territory in Donbas and also push against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:23.940
the Russian forces in the south, in Kherson.
The Ukrainians say they need more tanks in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:28.560
order to push forward. We're sitting here today,&nbsp;
we're speaking from the Berlin Foreign Policy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:33.840
Forum. Germany — a NATO member — is facing&nbsp;
criticism for not sending heavier military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.580
equipment to Ukraine, including Leopard&nbsp;
2 battle tanks. From your point of view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:50.040
should Germany provide the tanks to Ukraine now?
So Germany has provided and is providing a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:58.800
of essential equipment to Ukraine with advanced&nbsp;
air defense systems and other advanced weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:05.400
and also ammunition and artillery. Other&nbsp;
allies have provided similar types of support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:12.180
and other allies have also provided armored&nbsp;
vehicles and also a lot of extremely important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.820
different types of support to Ukraine.
But Mr. Secretary General, I would just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:22.440
like to underscore the urgency that we have&nbsp;
been hearing and I'd like to mention a quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:27.180
for example, from Latvia's defense minister, and&nbsp;
I'm going to read it out to you now. He says: "If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:34.200
Germany would give to Ukraine proportionately what&nbsp;
we have given to Ukraine, this war would be over."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:40.440
Does Germany, and other larger&nbsp;
EU states, need to do more now?&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:49.560
We all need to do more now. And that was the&nbsp;
message from the defense ministers when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:55.380
met last week. And we also saw significant new&nbsp;
announcements about more supplies. Then we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.380 --> 00:08:02.160
to realize that so far what we have done is to&nbsp;
mostly provide supplies or support to Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:08.760
by depleting our existing stocks. At some stage&nbsp;
we cannot continue to do so. So now we need also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:14.700
to ramp up production. And that was also the&nbsp;
other message from the NATO defense ministers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:21.600
was that we now engage directly with the industry&nbsp;
to be able to replenish our existing stocks of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:28.380
ammunition, of capabilities, so we can, on par,&nbsp;
both uphold our own deterrence and defense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:34.860
but also continue to provide support to Ukraine.
I want to talk a little bit more about the ramp-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:40.440
of production and the fact that that is a hole&nbsp;
right now. But first, Putin says that he will use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:45.120
and I'm quoting here, "all weapons systems&nbsp;
available to defend territory." This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:50.640
being seen as a thinly-veiled nuclear threat.&nbsp;
This week, NATO is conducting routine nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:57.660
drills. Russia's annual exercises are usually&nbsp;
taking place late in October. How high is your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:01.680
concern right now that the Russian drills could&nbsp;
be used to put nuclear weapons on high alert?&nbsp;

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:10.860
We are very closely monitoring what they are&nbsp;
doing and we are vigilant. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:15.300
especially when they now are going&nbsp;
to conduct a nuclear exercise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:24.660
Having said that, the risk of any nuclear&nbsp;
attack against Ukraine, the use of nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:30.300
weapons against Ukraine, is low. But of course,&nbsp;
the potential impact — the consequence — is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:37.320
big. So this is a risk we have to take seriously.&nbsp;
And we do so by conveying clearly to Russia that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:43.680
there will be severe consequences for Russia if&nbsp;
they use a nuclear weapon in Ukraine. And also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:54.180
reminding Russia of [the fact ] that a nuclear war&nbsp;
cannot be won and must never be fought. And we,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:58.740
of course, stay vigilant and monitor closely&nbsp;
what they do. So far, we haven't seen any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:03.360
changes in their nuclear posture and nuclear&nbsp;
readiness. But this is something we're constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:06.840
monitoring and especially when they&nbsp;
now conduct a nuclear exercise.&nbsp;

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.220
But given the attack on the Kerch Strait&nbsp;
Bridge in occupied Crimea in early October.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:16.380
and Ukraine's recent retaking of territory in the&nbsp;
country's south and east, do you think personally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:21.720
that a humiliated Putin is more&nbsp;
likely to lash out at this time?&nbsp;

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:31.320
I will be careful speculating about the different&nbsp;
risks and the different scenarios. We need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:38.340
prepared for all eventualities. And that's&nbsp;
exactly what we are. And at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:47.940
we cannot be intimidated or suffer or accept&nbsp;
blackmailing from Russia, because I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:56.160
this nuclear rhetoric and the threats we have seen&nbsp;
from Russia - the aim is, of course, to coerce us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:03.840
to blackmail us, to stop providing support&nbsp;
to Ukraine. But if we do that, then President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:09.240
Putin will win in Ukraine. And if he wins in&nbsp;
Ukraine, that will send a very dangerous message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:15.060
to all authoritarian leaders that when&nbsp;
they use military force in a brutal way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:20.460
violate international law, they will achieve&nbsp;
their goals. That will make us more vulnerable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:25.260
It will make the world more dangerous. And&nbsp;
therefore, yes, there are risks with all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:31.860
options in this conflict. But I think the risk&nbsp;
of letting President Putin win is much higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:39.180
than to continue to support Ukraine as we do.
You and NATO members, you have emphasized and I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:44.520
quoting here, "Russia must understand that nuclear&nbsp;
war can never be won and must never be fought."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:49.860
Do you have any assurances that that&nbsp;
message is being heard by Putin?&nbsp;

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:58.920
Yes, partly because it has been communicated&nbsp;
so many times from NATO allies, from also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:06.660
United States and other NATO allies that possesses&nbsp;
nuclear weapons, and from the whole alliance. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:13.920
actually, this is also something that Russia has&nbsp;
subscribed to. So this is a well-known message.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:16.980
You've warned of severe consequences. Such as?&nbsp;

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:27.720
We will never go into the exact way we will&nbsp;
respond, partly because that will depend so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:34.320
much on the kind of attack, the circumstances,&nbsp;
the context, but also because we will never give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:40.320
a potential adversary the privilege of knowing&nbsp;
exactly how we will respond. They know it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:44.460
be severe consequences. And we have repeated&nbsp;
that message several times over the last weeks.&nbsp;

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:48.600
But do all 30 NATO allies agree&nbsp;
on what the response would be?&nbsp;

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:59.760
All NATO allies agree on the seriousness&nbsp;
of any use of nuclear weapons. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:07.560
has been clearly conveyed from me and&nbsp;
from also many other NATO allies. Second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:14.340
I think we need to distinguish between the use of&nbsp;
weapons of mass destruction or nuclear weapons in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:21.180
Ukraine and any attack on a NATO ally. An attack&nbsp;
on a NATO ally will, of course, trigger Article 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:25.260
our collective defense clause, and to ensure&nbsp;
that there is no room for miscalculation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:30.540
for misunderstanding in Moscow about that, we have&nbsp;
increased our military presence in eastern part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:36.960
alliance, increased the readiness of our forces&nbsp;
to make sure that an attack on a NATO ally will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:41.580
of course, trigger a response from the&nbsp;
whole alliance. And we do that not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:45.480
provoke a conflict, but to prevent the&nbsp;
conflict, to preserve peace. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:50.040
fundamentally the same as NATO has done for&nbsp;
more than 70 years [as it] faced a nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:55.380
threat or a challenge from the Soviet Union.
Here's the response from Moscow. Putin's ally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.380 --> 00:14:00.240
Dmitry Medvedev, says that he thinks the NATO&nbsp;
military alliance would not risk a nuclear war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:05.760
and directly enter the Ukraine war, even if Moscow&nbsp;
struck Ukraine with nuclear weapons. Is he right?&nbsp;

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:13.500
He is not right. Because what we have&nbsp;
stated clearly that is that there will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:20.640
be severe consequences, but we have not&nbsp;
lined out or gone into details what kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:26.640
consequences there will be. And of course,&nbsp;
we have many ways to respond. And and and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:30.960
that's exactly what we have communicated.
While you warned, though, Moscow of severe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:36.720
consequences, French President Emmanuel Macron&nbsp;
says that French — a French nuclear response to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:41.880
Russia using its own atomic arsenal against&nbsp;
Ukraine or the region — is off the table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:46.020
That statement wasn't very helpful&nbsp;
for your deterrence, was it?&nbsp;

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:55.440
But we have different ways of reacting. It&nbsp;
doesn't have to be any use of nuclear weapons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.440 --> 00:15:03.540
What we have stated again and again is that the&nbsp;
circumstances in which NATO would consider the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:11.760
use of a nuclear weapon remains very remote, but&nbsp;
that does not take away the possibility of NATO to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:18.180
respond, and NATO allies to respond, if there is&nbsp;
a use of nuclear weapon by Russia against Ukraine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:24.600
And again, if there is any attack against a NATO&nbsp;
ally, we have the whole alliance and Article 5 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:28.740
our collective defense clause. And the purpose&nbsp;
of that is to prevent that from happening.&nbsp;

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:34.560
Russia says that western states' support of&nbsp;
Ukraine — you've mentioned what NATO members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:40.500
are sending to Ukraine — help make them a direct&nbsp;
party to the conflict. With NATO members supplying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:45.900
the majority of support, isn't it going to be&nbsp;
difficult for members to not be seen as a direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:53.520
party? How are you toeing that line going forward?
No, because we are not party to the conflict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.520 --> 00:16:01.680
Meaning that we don't have forces on the ground.&nbsp;
We don't participate in the fighting. But what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:07.140
do is that we provide support to Ukraine. Ukraine&nbsp;
has the right for self-defense. This is a right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:13.560
which is enshrined in the UN treaty. Every nation&nbsp;
has the right to defend themselves against a war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:19.020
of aggression, a brutal attack, as we have seen&nbsp;
against Ukraine. And NATO allies are helping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:23.940
Ukraine to uphold that right for self-defense.&nbsp;
But that doesn't make us a party to the conflict.&nbsp;

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:27.780
I'd like to ask you about NATO enlargement,&nbsp;
and in particular, Ukrainian President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:34.800
Volodymyr Zelenskyy announced a bid for fast-track&nbsp;
membership of NATO. He pointed to the examples of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:39.960
Finland and Sweden. Will you agree to fast-track&nbsp;
NATO's bid [from Ukraine] in the next 12 months?&nbsp;

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:48.360
It's nothing new that Ukraine has the ambition of&nbsp;
joining NATO. NATO's position remains unchanged.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:52.440
NATO's door is open. We have demonstrated that&nbsp;
over the last years with North Macedonia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:59.220
Montenegro, but also with Finland and Sweden. And&nbsp;
of course, we also strongly conveyed the message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:03.660
that every nation has the right to choose some own&nbsp;
path, including, of course, Ukraine. At the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:09.120
the day, it will be for the 30 NATO allies and&nbsp;
Ukraine to decide on membership. Russia doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:13.500
have any say in that. Our main focus now is...
Right.&nbsp;

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:20.340
...on ensuring that Ukraine wins the&nbsp;
war and therefore all our focus is on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:23.400
providing support to Ukraine.
But the question is, do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:28.860
personally support NATO — Ukraine's&nbsp;
application for fast-track membership.

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:39.240
I personally stand by the decisions we have made,&nbsp;
which is on saying that NATO's door remains open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:48.060
and that Ukraine one day will be a member of the&nbsp;
alliance. But the short-term, the priority now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:57.960
is on providing support to Ukraine to enable&nbsp;
them to make more gains on the battlefield and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:03.480
then to ensure that we can end the war in a way&nbsp;
that ensures that Ukraine remains a sovereign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:08.040
independent nation in Europe. That's a&nbsp;
precondition for any discussion about membership.&nbsp;

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:12.360
You have, though, and I'd just like to quote you&nbsp;
here, you have supported Finland and Sweden's bid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:18.780
You came out and you said "this would&nbsp;
be an historic moment." Why do you dodge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:23.700
clearly answering whether or not you&nbsp;
support Ukraine in a fast-track bid?&nbsp;

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:29.400
Because I strongly believe that Ukraine is in a&nbsp;
different position than Finland and Sweden are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:37.260
Finland and Sweden are long-term partners of NATO.&nbsp;
We have consensus in the alliance on inviting them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:45.660
to become members. We made that decision at&nbsp;
the summit in Madrid in May. What we see in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:53.040
Ukraine is a war going on and the top priority is&nbsp;
therefore to provide military support to Ukraine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:59.160
as we do as NATO's allies and partners. And&nbsp;
this military support is unprecedented. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:04.860
it's really helped the Ukrainians to now&nbsp;
make gains on the battlefield. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:09.120
the victories on the battlefield is something&nbsp;
that belongs to the brave Ukrainian soldiers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:14.400
But without the support from NATO allies, they&nbsp;
would have not been able to make these gains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:20.220
and to push the Russian invading forces&nbsp;
back, as I've seen over the last weeks.&nbsp;

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:26.220
If we look back in 2008, NATO pledged that Ukraine&nbsp;
would eventually become an alliance member.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:30.720
Ukraine, of course, has this on paper. Did NATO&nbsp;
give false hopes to Ukraine all those years ago?&nbsp;

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:41.760
No, this is still the ambition of the alliance.&nbsp;
But the focus has been since 2008 on reforms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:51.660
on capacity building, on supporting Ukraine in&nbsp;
moving towards NATO membership. As I also know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.660 --> 00:20:00.300
that EU members have been working with Ukraine,&nbsp;
moving towards EU membership, but of course since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:08.220
2014 we have been in a situation where Russia&nbsp;
has annexed Crimea and controlled the eastern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:15.240
part of Donbas. So the main focus has then been&nbsp;
to provide military support to Ukraine and NATO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:20.100
allies — like especially the United States, the&nbsp;
United Kingdom, Canada —have trained and equipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:25.500
the Ukrainian forces not since February,&nbsp;
but since 2014. And that has ensured that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:29.700
Ukrainian armed forces are much bigger, much&nbsp;
better trained, much better led, much better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:35.820
equipped now than in 2014. And this is making a&nbsp;
huge difference on the battlefield as we speak.&nbsp;

00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:40.980
Let's turn briefly to your strategic concept&nbsp;
and to future threats. At the Chinese Communist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:44.460
Party Congress, President Xi Jinping said&nbsp;
that China would use, and I'm quoting here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:49.320
"all necessary measures to secure Taiwan."&nbsp;
Is the next conflict for NATO with China?&nbsp;

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:59.280
Well, Taiwan is not a NATO member, so our&nbsp;
collective defense clause doesn't apply for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:02.400
Taiwan. But what we see is...
But Mr. Secretary General,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:06.840
how would NATO respond, if China were to&nbsp;
invade Taiwan? How would NATO's respond?&nbsp;

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:14.880
I'm not going to outline exactly how NATO&nbsp;
will respond. What we have stated clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:20.640
and that's for the first time in our strategic&nbsp;
concept, is that the rise of China matters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:25.080
to our security. And it is a challenge for our&nbsp;
values, for our interests, and for our security.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:31.560
And that is because China is now investing&nbsp;
heavily in new modern military capabilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:38.460
also long-range missiles, nuclear weapons. We&nbsp;
see how they don't share our values in the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:44.880
they crack down on democratic voices, forces&nbsp;
in Hong Kong, the minorities they abuse, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:53.700
also how they coerce countries in the region. So&nbsp;
all of this makes it important for us to address&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.700 --> 00:22:00.720
the rise of China, not by isolating China. China&nbsp;
is important also to engage with, and therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:07.980
we are both addressing the security challenges,&nbsp;
but also the need to engage with China as we do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:11.640
And I met recently with the Chinese foreign&nbsp;
minister and that was my main message to him.&nbsp;

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:15.720
The head of the British cyber intelligence&nbsp;
agency, he put it this way. He describes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:21.300
the security threat posed by Russia as affecting&nbsp;
the weather, while China's affects the climate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:27.480
What do you think there is to be learned&nbsp;
from allies' support of Ukraine against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:33.240
the Russian invasion, should China move&nbsp;
to invade or to illegally annex territory?&nbsp;

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:41.700
That's always unacceptable that one country tried&nbsp;
to grab land from another country. And that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:47.520
reason why we have reacted so strongly&nbsp;
against the Russian invasion of Ukraine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:55.500
because this is the biggest land grab since the&nbsp;
Second World War, world war in Europe. And also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.500 --> 00:23:05.220
why we conveyed to China that, of course, they&nbsp;
should be able to at least condemn the illegal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:12.000
war of Russia against Ukraine. And also one of&nbsp;
the reasons why we support Ukraine is to send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:18.240
a message to all other authoritarian leaders&nbsp;
that we don't accept this kind of behavior.&nbsp;

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:23.880
I'd like to also ask you, you said last week that&nbsp;
when we look at the situation in Ukraine, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:25.800
allies have had to dip into their&nbsp;
own arsenals in order to send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:30.240
supplies there. Were NATO members too complacent?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:35.580
Because if you look just at the first half of&nbsp;
the year, only nine members were spending 2%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:43.380
of GDP or more on defense. Now, it seems to be a&nbsp;
bit of a scramble in order to get stockpiles and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.380 --> 00:23:49.380
increase production capacities. And it's coming&nbsp;
amid hard economic times. Can it stay a priority?&nbsp;

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:56.400
I have, we have pushed for more defense&nbsp;
investments across the alliance for many years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.400 --> 00:24:00.420
and of course we would like to see even&nbsp;
more defense spending. Having said that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.420 --> 00:24:06.720
what we have seen since 2014 is the biggest&nbsp;
reinforcement of NATO's collective defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:13.200
since the end of the Cold War. Until 2014, NATO&nbsp;
allies were cutting defense spending. Since 2014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:19.980
all NATO allies have increased defense spending&nbsp;
with hundreds of billions of extra US dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:24.300
and we have increased the readiness of&nbsp;
forces. For the first time in history we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.300 --> 00:24:29.100
have battlegroups in the eastern part of the&nbsp;
alliance and all of this were triggered by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:36.300
Russian annexation of Crimea and the control over&nbsp;
eastern Donbas. So we were actually prepared when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:42.420
the invasion happened in February. We actually&nbsp;
warned against it since last fall, shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:49.980
intelligence depicting exactly the Russian plans&nbsp;
to invade Ukraine. And on the day of the invasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:54.660
we activated our defense plans and further&nbsp;
increased our military presence in the east.&nbsp;

00:24:54.660 --> 00:24:59.040
Briefly, before we go, Secretary General, must&nbsp;
Putin go for there to be a resolution in Ukraine?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:06.480
Yes or no?
Again, I will not... But you will not force me to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:12.600
give a yes or no answer to that. What we will do&nbsp;
is that we will respond to the actions of Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:17.640
regardless of who is in charge there. And we will&nbsp;
continue to call on them to end this war. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:21.960
President Putin that started this war, and it's&nbsp;
President Putin who could end this war tomorrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.960 --> 00:25:27.300
by withdrawing his forces. We have to remember&nbsp;
that if President Putin and Russia stops fighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.300 --> 00:25:30.660
there will be peace. If President&nbsp;
Zelenskyy and Ukraine stops fighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:35.880
Ukraine will cease to exist as an independent&nbsp;
nation. And we don't want that to happen. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:39.900
exactly why we provide support to Ukraine.
NATO's secretary general, Jens Stoltenberg.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:43.260
Thank you very much for joining us on&nbsp;
Conflict Zone. Thank you for your time.

